{"id":"deecb408-8f82-4abd-85f4-12e45edf0a09","recipient":"a228afbf-3d9a-4a60-8ce6-ace5f878390a","sender":"b0b6fc47-18db-4c28-91e7-af9ea5ee9dcd","priority":"MESSAGE_PRIORITY_HIGH","timestamp":"2026-09-14T09:57:12.782270800Z","renderDetails":{"messageTitle":"Message from Formatter (research)"},"content":"--- **WRIST SPECIAL TESTS** --- **I CARPAL TUNNEL SYNDROME TESTS** --- **1 PHALENS TEST** Purpose To assess Carpal Tunnel Syndrome Median nerve compression Procedure • Ask the patient to flex both wrists maximally • Dorsum of both hands are pressed together • Hold for 60 seconds Positive Sign Pain tingling or numbness in the thumb index middle and lateral half of ring finger Indicates Carpal Tunnel Syndrome --- **2 REVERSE PHALENS TEST PRAYER TEST** Purpose To assess Carpal Tunnel Syndrome Procedure • Ask the patient to press both palms together • Maintain maximum wrist extension for 60 seconds Positive Sign Pain or paresthesia in the median nerve distribution Indicates Carpal Tunnel Syndrome --- **3 TINELS SIGN WRIST** Purpose To assess Median nerve irritation Procedure • Tap gently over the median nerve at the carpal tunnel Positive Sign Tingling or electric shock sensation in the median nerve distribution Indicates Carpal Tunnel Syndrome --- **II TENDON PATHOLOGY TEST** --- **4 FINKELSTEINS TEST** Purpose To assess De Quervains Tenosynovitis Procedure • Patient places the thumb inside the fist • Fingers wrap around the thumb • Examiner passively deviates the wrist towards the ulnar side Positive Sign Pain over the radial styloid Indicates De Quervains Tenosynovitis --- **III LIGAMENT STABILITY TEST** --- **5 WATSON SCAPHOID SHIFT TEST** Purpose To assess Scapholunate ligament instability Procedure • Apply pressure over the scaphoid tubercle • Move the wrist from ulnar deviation to radial deviation Positive Sign Pain or palpable clunk Indicates Scapholunate ligament injury --- **IV THUMB TEST** --- **6 GRIND TEST** Purpose To assess 1st Carpometacarpal CMC joint osteoarthritis Procedure • Apply axial compression to the thumb • Simultaneously rotate the thumb metacarpal Positive Sign Pain or crepitus Indicates 1st CMC joint osteoarthritis --- **I TESTS FOR BONE LIGAMENT CAPSULE JOINT INSTABILITY** --- **1 ULNAR FOVEA TEST** Purpose To assess Triangular Fibrocartilage Complex TFCC injury Procedure • Press the examiners thumb into the ulnar fovea between the ulnar styloid and flexor carpi ulnaris tendon Positive Sign Pain or tenderness over the ulnar fovea Indicates TFCC injury or ulnotriquetral ligament injury --- **2 ULNO MENISCO TRIQUETRAL DORSAL GLIDE TEST** Purpose To assess ulnotriquetral ligament and TFCC pathology Procedure • Stabilize the distal ulna • Apply a dorsal glide to the triquetrum while assessing pain and movement Positive Sign Pain or excessive movement Indicates Ulnotriquetral ligament injury or TFCC lesion --- **3 BUNNELL LITTLER TEST** Purpose To differentiate intrinsic muscle tightness from joint capsule tightness Procedure • Flex the PIP joint with the MCP joint extended • Repeat with the MCP joint flexed Positive Sign • PIP flexion improves when MCP is flexed Intrinsic muscle tightness • PIP flexion remains limited Capsular tightness Indicates Intrinsic muscle contracture or capsular restriction","sourceMetadata":{"tool":{"conversationId":"b0b6fc47-18db-4c28-91e7-af9ea5ee9dcd","stepIndex":4,"toolCall":{"id":"call_6188962","name":"send_message","argumentsJson":"{\"Message\":\"--- **WRIST SPECIAL TESTS** --- **I CARPAL TUNNEL SYNDROME TESTS** --- **1 PHALENS TEST** Purpose To assess Carpal Tunnel Syndrome Median nerve compression Procedure • Ask the patient to flex both wrists maximally • Dorsum of both hands are pressed together • Hold for 60 seconds Positive Sign Pain tingling or numbness in the thumb index middle and lateral half of ring finger Indicates Carpal Tunnel Syndrome --- **2 REVERSE PHALENS TEST PRAYER TEST** Purpose To assess Carpal Tunnel Syndrome Procedure • Ask the patient to press both palms together • Maintain maximum wrist extension for 60 seconds Positive Sign Pain or paresthesia in the median nerve distribution Indicates Carpal Tunnel Syndrome --- **3 TINELS SIGN WRIST** Purpose To assess Median nerve irritation Procedure • Tap gently over the median nerve at the carpal tunnel Positive Sign Tingling or electric shock sensation in the median nerve distribution Indicates Carpal Tunnel Syndrome --- **II TENDON PATHOLOGY TEST** --- **4 FINKELSTEINS TEST** Purpose To assess De Quervains Tenosynovitis Procedure • Patient places the thumb inside the fist • Fingers wrap around the thumb • Examiner passively deviates the wrist towards the ulnar side Positive Sign Pain over the radial styloid Indicates De Quervains Tenosynovitis --- **III LIGAMENT STABILITY TEST** --- **5 WATSON SCAPHOID SHIFT TEST** Purpose To assess Scapholunate ligament instability Procedure • Apply pressure over the scaphoid tubercle • Move the wrist from ulnar deviation to radial deviation Positive Sign Pain or palpable clunk Indicates Scapholunate ligament injury --- **IV THUMB TEST** --- **6 GRIND TEST** Purpose To assess 1st Carpometacarpal CMC joint osteoarthritis Procedure • Apply axial compression to the thumb • Simultaneously rotate the thumb metacarpal Positive Sign Pain or crepitus Indicates 1st CMC joint osteoarthritis --- **I TESTS FOR BONE LIGAMENT CAPSULE JOINT INSTABILITY** --- **1 ULNAR FOVEA TEST** Purpose To assess Triangular Fibrocartilage Complex TFCC injury Procedure • Press the examiners thumb into the ulnar fovea between the ulnar styloid and flexor carpi ulnaris tendon Positive Sign Pain or tenderness over the ulnar fovea Indicates TFCC injury or ulnotriquetral ligament injury --- **2 ULNO MENISCO TRIQUETRAL DORSAL GLIDE TEST** Purpose To assess ulnotriquetral ligament and TFCC pathology Procedure • Stabilize the distal ulna • Apply a dorsal glide to the triquetrum while assessing pain and movement Positive Sign Pain or excessive movement Indicates Ulnotriquetral ligament injury or TFCC lesion --- **3 BUNNELL LITTLER TEST** Purpose To differentiate intrinsic muscle tightness from joint capsule tightness Procedure • Flex the PIP joint with the MCP joint extended • Repeat with the MCP joint flexed Positive Sign • PIP flexion improves when MCP is flexed Intrinsic muscle tightness • PIP flexion remains limited Capsular tightness Indicates Intrinsic muscle contracture or capsular restriction\",\"Recipient\":\"a228afbf-3d9a-4a60-8ce6-ace5f878390a\",\"toolAction\":\"Sending extracted tests\",\"toolSummary\":\"Sending extracted text to parent\"}","thinkingSignature":""}}}}